<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>TableTop.Games</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>TableTop.Games</RootNamespace>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\TableTop.Core\TableTop.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    JSON card decks were removed in two steps: the 95 files in 1.18.0, and the
    loader that read them (JsonDeckLoader, JsonDataPaths, ModePresentationLoader,
    DeckBulkExporter) in 1.19.0. Every mode now builds its deck from its in-code
    bank, directly — no probe, no fallback, no diagnostic.

    Verified before deleting the files, not assumed: of 95 deck files, only 11
    were ever loaded — the other 84 were orphaned, matching no mode's
    DeckResourceName. For all 11 live decks, a card-by-card comparison against
    the C# fallback found zero content unique to either side.

    What 1.18.0 left behind, and 1.19.0 removed: 92 LoadOrFallback call sites
    referencing files that could no longer exist. Each cost an embedded-resource
    probe plus a filesystem walk to the root on every deck build, and each fired
    a "deck file missing" diagnostic — a signal that had become unconditional and
    could therefore no longer mean anything.

    User-supplied content is a SEPARATE and still-live feature: JsonGameMode
    reads decks the player drops in at runtime, through JsonCardProvider,
    CardDeckFile and DeckFileValidator in TableTop.Core. None of that was
    touched. DeckExporter still emits a deck file.

    KEEPING THE ANDROID LESSON, because it cost real debugging and applies to
    any file-based content added here later: <Content> does NOT work on
    Android. An APK is a zip, there is no directory "next to the assembly", and
    Assembly.Location returns an empty string. Under the debugger, MAUI
    fast-deployment puts loose files on the device so paths resolve and
    everything looks fine; in a packaged build it doesn't, and every deck
    silently fell back to its built-in bank. Use <EmbeddedResource> — it works
    identically on every platform and survives trimming and AOT.
  -->
</Project>
